  "content": {
    "title": "NASA's rollout of the Artemis II rocket complete after wind delay",
    "text": "NASA's rollout of the Artemis II rocket complete after wind delay Video Player is loading.Current Time 0:00Duration 0:00Remaining Time 0:00✕ THERE IT IS. NASA’S BIG MOON ROCKET BACK ON THE PAD AT THE KENNEDY SPACE CENTER. THE SPACE LAUNCH SYSTEM COMPLETED. IT’S VERY SLOW. FOUR MILE TREK JUST BEFORE NOON TODAY. AND NOW THE FINAL PREPS BEFORE LAUNCH. OFFICIALLY SET FOR APRIL 1ST. WESH 2’S MEAGHAN MORIARTY LIVE AT KENNEDY SPACE CENTER. MEAGHAN. THOSE PREPS WILL BE MUCH DIFFERENT THIS TIME AROUND. AFTER A FOUR HOUR DELAY LAST NIGHT BECAUSE OF THE WIND, ARTEMIS TWO IS NOW BACK WHERE IT BELONGS ON LAUNCH PAD 39 B, BUT THE WORK IS JUST GETTING STARTED. THERE’S A LOT THAT STILL NEEDS TO BE DONE IN ORDER TO ENSURE AN APRIL LAUNCH DATE. THE COUNTDOWN IS ON TO GET ARTEMIS TWO READY TO LIFT OFF FROM THE SPACE COAST. THE 322 FOOT SLS ROCKET MADE THE SLOW AND STEADY FOUR MILE TREK TO THE LAUNCH PAD JUST BEFORE 1 A.M. FRIDAY, AND NOW THAT IT’S THERE, THEY CAN DO THE TEN DAYS OR SO OF WORK TO GET IT READY. AND THAT INVOLVES CONNECTING ALL OF THE ROCKET SIDE UMBILICALS TO THE GROUND. SIGNED UMBILICALS. THEY NEED POWER. THEY NEED COMPUTERS. CREWS WILL ALSO NEED TO POWER UP THE ROCKET AND ORION CAPSULE TO ENSURE ALL THE SYSTEMS ARE WORKING PROPERLY. THE ROCKET WAS ORIGINALLY BROUGHT OUT TO THE PAD IN JANUARY, BUT HYDROGEN LEAKS DELAYED A FEBRUARY LAUNCH DATE AND A HELIUM FLOW ISSUE FORCED NASA TO ROLL IT BACK TO THE VEHICLE ASSEMBLY BUILDING FOR REPAIRS. WE DON’T KNOW IF THAT ROCKET IS GOING TO LIFT OFF UNTIL IT ACTUALLY LIFTS OFF. WE GOT TO SEE WHAT HAPPENS WITH THE HYDROGEN AS THE HYDROGEN AND THEY ALL BEEN RESOLVED? WE’RE HOPEFUL, BUT SINCE THEY’RE NOT DOING A TETRIS, WE WON’T KNOW UNTIL THAT MOMENT. TYPICALLY, WET DRESS REHEARSALS, A CRITICAL FUELING TEST BEFORE THE ACTUAL LAUNCH. BUT EVERY TIME THEY FUEL THE ROCKET, IT PUTS A LOT OF WEAR AND TEAR ON THE HARDWARE. NASA HAS ALREADY FUELED IT THREE TIMES PRIOR, SO THEY’RE SKIPPING WET DRESS THIS TIME AROUND INSTEAD. THE NEXT FUELING WILL BE WHEN THE ROCKET IS SET TO GO TO THE MOON. LET’S BE OPTIMISTIC THEY’LL GET IT DONE. AND SO APRIL 1ST IS THE DATE THAT THEY ARE TARGETING. THEY HAVE SIX LAUNCH OPPORTUNITIES IN APRIL. SO FROM APRIL 1ST TO APRIL 6TH, THEY CAN LAUNCH ANY OF THOSE DAYS. NOW, IF THEY CAN’T LAUNCH ON APRIL 1ST, FOR SOME REASON, THEY’RE NOT GOING TO THEN LAUNCH THEY CAN’T LAUNCH EVERY SINGLE DAY. THEY WILL HAVE THE CREW REST FOR ABOUT 48 HOURS BEFOR NASA's rollout of the Artemis II rocket complete after wind delay Updated: 4:27 PM EDT Mar 20, 2026 Editorial Standards ⓘ NASA's Artemis II rocket rollout is complete after a wind delay overnight, according to officials.The process was supposed to start at 8 p.m. on Thursday, moving the rocket from the Vehicle Assembly Building to the launch pad at Kennedy Space Center. Officials hope for liftoff between April 1 and April 6, following the resolution of an issue with the flow of helium in the rocket's upper stage.The rollout process, which could take between eight and 12 hours, marks the second time NASA has undertaken this procedure with Artemis II.NASA has confirmed that the helium issue has been fixed, and the Artemis II team is currently in quarantine in Houston. During a recent news conference, NASA officials said, \"Again, while I am comfortable and the agency is comfortable with targeting April 1st as our first opportunity. Just keep in mind we still have work to go. There are still things that need to be done within the VAB and out at the pad. And as always, we'll always be guided by what the hardware is telling us, and we will launch when we're ready.\" CAPE CANAVERAL, FL —NASA's Artemis II rocket rollout is complete after a wind delay overnight, according to officials.The process was supposed to start at 8 p.m. on Thursday, moving the rocket from the Vehicle Assembly Building to the launch pad at Kennedy Space Center. Officials hope for liftoff between April 1 and April 6, following the resolution of an issue with the flow of helium in the rocket's upper stage. The rollout process, which could take between eight and 12 hours, marks the second time NASA has undertaken this procedure with Artemis II. This content is imported from Twitter. You may be able to find the same content in another format, or you may be able to find more information, at their web site. Let's roll! 🚀NASA’s SLS rocket and Orion spacecraft for the @NASAArtemis II mission are making their way to Launch Pad 39B. The overnight journey is expected to take up to 12 hours. https://t.co/JoIOVpXagV— NASA's Kennedy Space Center (@NASAKennedy) March 20, 2026 NASA has confirmed that the helium issue has been fixed, and the Artemis II team is currently in quarantine in Houston. During a recent news conference, NASA officials said, \"Again, while I am comfortable and the agency is comfortable with targeting April 1st as our first opportunity. Just keep in mind we still have work to go. There are still things that need to be done within the VAB and out at the pad. And as always, we'll always be guided by what the hardware is telling us, and we will launch when we're ready.\"",
